<h2 id="what-is-gohighlevel">🔍 What Is GoHighLevel?</h2>

GoHighLevel is an all-in-one marketing and CRM platform originally built for agencies but increasingly adopted by multi-location business operators. It consolidates CRM, email marketing, SMS campaigns, reputation management, appointment scheduling, funnel building, and pipeline tracking into a single platform.

The platform uses a "sub-account" architecture that maps perfectly to multi-location operations. Each location gets its own sub-account with separate pipelines, automations, and reporting — while you maintain centralized oversight and templating from a parent account.

What makes GoHighLevel different from alternatives like HubSpot or Salesforce is the pricing model. There are no per-seat fees. No per-contact fees that scale up. Whether you have 3 locations or 300, you're paying the same flat monthly rate. For operators who've watched their HubSpot bill triple as they scaled, this alone is worth the investigation.

<h2 id="our-experience">💼 Our Experience Testing GoHighLevel Across Locations</h2>

Our team has managed thousands of customer journeys and SKUs across restaurant groups, fitness franchises, and service businesses. We evaluated GoHighLevel specifically for its multi-location capabilities after one team member's 12-location salon group was paying $3,100/month across Mailchimp, Podium, Calendly, and separate CRM tools.

We deployed GoHighLevel across 47 locations in three different verticals over 14 months:

**Restaurant Group (18 locations):** Used primarily for reputation management, SMS marketing, and customer reactivation campaigns. The missed-call text-back feature alone recovered an estimated 340 reservations monthly across the group.

**Fitness Franchise (21 locations):** Full CRM deployment with lead tracking, membership pipelines, and automated nurture sequences. We tracked 12,400+ leads through the system with location-specific attribution.

**Home Services (8 locations):** Appointment booking, review requests, and estimate follow-up automation. This was our most complex implementation due to varying service areas and technician scheduling.

The honest assessment: implementation took 6-8 weeks to get right. This is not plug-and-play software. But once the automations were built and templates established, location managers needed minimal training to use day-to-day features.

